    "4d695bcc-78d0-481c-9e1c-d5879391c8b8": {"id":"4d695bcc-78d0-481c-9e1c-d5879391c8b8","project_id":48873,"key":221328848,"version":null,"meta":{},"children":[],"deleted_at":null,"created_at":"2025-07-04T03:54:11.846Z","updated_at":"2025-07-04T03:54:11.846Z","props":{"useBackgroundControls":true,"content":"\u003cdiv class=\"chameleon-text-align-left\" data-type=\"textAlignLeft\"\u003e\u003cul class=\"chameleon-ul\" data-type=\"bulleted-list\"\u003e\u003cp data-type=\"paragraph\"\u003eChange the units number in the “Move” block to 10. This means that every time you click the Elephant, it will move forward by 10 units. You can play around with this number to see how far you want the elephant to move each time the elephant is clicked.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-type=\"paragraph\"\u003eHere and for the remainder of this module, we will leave the duration to “1” and easing to “linear.” We will explain the two in more detail in Module 2. For now, just note that duration is the total time that an object moves, and easing controls how the speed of the movement changes over time (does it move at the same speed the whole time or does it start off slower and gets faster as time goes on?).\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003c/div\u003e","className":"text-align-center","enableTextToSpeechBtn":true},"type":"Text"}, 
